# The machine-scoped ArtLight host runs as the unprivileged "artlight"
# account. Grant it virtual input devices through a dedicated group instead of
# the broad "input" group, which would also expose every physical input event.
# Deliberately NO TAG+="uaccess" on these three, and it must not come back.
#
# It reads as harmless and it is not. uaccess makes logind grant the LOGGED-IN user an
# ACL on the node, and acl_set leaves the GROUP class empty - the real mode becomes 0600
# even though the rule says MODE="0660". The artlight-uinput group above then grants
# nothing, so the host cannot open these at all.
#
# Measured on a real machine, 2026-10-06: with uaccess present, `ls -l` still printed
# crw-rw---- and looked perfectly correct, because ls shows the ACL MASK in the group
# position; `getfacl` showed the truth, group::--- . Every virtual input device failed
# to create - "Unable to create virtual mouse: Permission denied", same for the
# keyboard, the touch screen, the pen tablet and both gamepads - so an unshared mouse,
# keyboard or gamepad simply did not exist for a client to use. Proved by isolation:
# the same account and group in a clean sandbox were denied, and chmod 0660 on the node
# made the write succeed and every device appear.
#
# These devices belong to the SERVICE, not to whoever happens to be logged in at the
# seat, which is exactly why the dedicated group exists. uaccess hands them to the
# human and takes them away from the service.
# OWNER is the belt, and it is here on purpose.
#
# Another package on the machine can put the uaccess tag back whatever this file says
# (Steam's and KDE Connect's rules both tag /dev/uinput), and udev has no way to veto
# them from here - a tag cannot be unset by simply not setting it. So the group grant
# above is one tag away from being dead.
#
# Ownership cannot be taken away that way. The ACL entry for "the owner" always applies
# to whoever owns the node, whichever order udev and logind do things in, so making the
# host the owner guarantees it can open the device no matter what anybody else's tag
# does. Nothing is lost by it: the seat user's own ACL entry is separate and still
# issued, so KDE Connect and Steam Input keep working exactly as before.
KERNEL=="uinput", SUBSYSTEM=="misc", OWNER="artlight", GROUP="artlight-uinput", MODE="0660", OPTIONS+="static_node=uinput"
KERNEL=="uhid", SUBSYSTEM=="misc", OWNER="artlight", GROUP="artlight-uinput", MODE="0660", OPTIONS+="static_node=uhid"

# uaccess stays on this one, for now and deliberately. It is the same trap as the two
# above, and the group grant here is probably just as dead - but nothing in the host's
# own source opens this node, while the only userspace opener in the tree is
# tests/integration/probe_ds5_gadget_lifecycle.cpp, which opens it O_RDWR as an ordinary
# user and so depends on uaccess to do it. Fixing a device that has never been reported
# broken at the cost of a test that would break silently is not a trade worth making in
# the same change. Revisit with the same getfacl proof if a DS5 problem is ever reported.
KERNEL=="vibeshine-ds5", SUBSYSTEM=="misc", GROUP="artlight-uinput", MODE="0660", TAG+="uaccess"

# A keyboard shared into this machine over USB/IP, so the host can watch for the
# combination that ends a stream. Same group and the same reasoning as the three
# above: the devices that arrived over the virtual host controller, and never the
# keyboards built into the machine.
#
# A device attached this way is enumerated below vhci_hcd.N, so its sysfs path
# contains that component and a built-in keyboard's does not. Measured on a real
# machine: /devices/platform/vhci_hcd.0, while its own keyboards sit on
# /devices/pci0000:00/.../input0 and /devices/platform/PNP0C0E:00/input/input1 -
# none of them match. Proved with udevadm test before this line was written, and
# the machine's own keyboards are still root:input after it is installed.
#
# The digit is required because that is the only name the kernel creates. The
# reader in src/platform/linux/quit_hotkey_device.cpp carries the identical test
# and the two must stay in step: a reader stricter than this rule is handed a
# device it then refuses to open, so the feature does nothing and the log says
# nothing.
#
# 70- on purpose: 50-udev-default.rules sets GROUP="input" on input nodes, and a
# later file wins, so this takes effect over it.
SUBSYSTEM=="input", KERNEL=="event*", DEVPATH=="*/vhci_hcd.[0-9]*", GROUP="artlight-uinput", MODE="0660"
